# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LINE ENDINGS. ONE KIND, EVERYWHERE.
#
# ---- WHY THIS MATTERS MORE HERE THAN IN MOST PROJECTS ----
#
# Windows ends a line with two characters (CR LF). Linux uses one (LF). Git on
# Windows defaults to converting between them, which is where the wall of
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings comes from. In most projects it is
# noise and nothing else.
#
# ⚠️ THIS PROJECT IS CHECKED BY THIRTY-ONE SCRIPTS THAT READ SOURCE AS TEXT.
# They count words in a lesson, balance brackets, match patterns at the end of
# a line, and read call sites as strings. A stray CR is an extra character on
# every line, and it lands in exactly those places.
#
# And the two machines would disagree. WITHOUT THIS FILE:
#
#   · Gigi's Windows copy gets CRLF the next time git touches it
#   · Netlify checks out LF, because Netlify is Linux
#   · so `npm run check` on her machine and `npm run check` on the build server
#     are reading DIFFERENT BYTES
#
# That is v3.73 waiting to happen again — the version where eighteen of thirty
# checks turned out never to have run on Windows at all, while the suite looked
# green for months. A check that passes on one machine and fails on the other
# is bad; a check that passes on both while reading different text is worse,
# because nothing tells you.
#
# `eol=lf` settles it: LF in the repository AND in the working copy, on both
# machines. The warnings stop because there is no conversion left to warn about.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Everything git decides is text gets LF, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Said explicitly for the files the checks actually parse, so the rule does not
# depend on git's guess about what counts as text.
*.js    text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf

# ⚠️ EXCEPT THIS ONE. START-PETAL-PESTLE.bat is run by Windows itself, and a
# batch file with Unix line endings can fail in ways that are hard to read —
# it is the file Azianna's setup depends on, so it keeps CRLF deliberately.
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.pdf   binary
